"Non-transitive Dice - Microsoft Research (https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/non-transitive-dice/)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/non-transitive-dice/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 60\nL0: cite0†Image L1: # Non-transitive Dice\nL2: \nL3: Embracing Uncertainty\nL4: \nL5: Scientists have worked for decades to try to create intelligence in computers. Traditional approaches relied on hand-crafted solutions and had limited applicability. New artificial intelligence techniques developed at Microsoft and other institutions around the world are based on computers that can learn for themselves by analysing large sets of data.\nL6: The key to learning from examples is to recognise that real-world data is full of complexity, ambiguity, and uncertainty. Computers can be programmed to handle these challenges by using a branch of mathematics called probability theory. By embracing the mathematics of uncertainty, we are able to create machines that can learn from data and apply these to problems ranging from medical data analysis to the recognition of objects in images.\nL7: To learn more about Microsoft’s work in artificial intelligence, visit aka.ms/artificialintelligence\nL8: \nL9: About the dice\nL10: \nL11: First of all, let us examine the dice in detail. Here we have ‘flattened’ the dice to show the numbers on each of the faces.\nL12: cite1†Image: dice_illustration We see that the choice of numbers is unusual. But in all other respects these are normal, fair dice. If we pick two of the dice and roll them, we can see which comes up with the higher number. We will say that the die with the higher number is the winner. Notice that each of the numbers 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6 appears on only one of the dice, which means that when we roll one die against another, there can never be a draw.\nL13: First, suppose you roll the orange and yellow dice. Two thirds of the time the yellow die will come up with a higher number than the orange die. We say that the probability that the yellow die will win is two thirds. If we make a large number of such rolls and keep track of which die wins, then there is a very high chance that the yellow die will win more often than the orange die.\nL14: Likewise, two thirds of the time the blue die will win against the yellow die, and two thirds of the time the green die will win against the blue die. So, yellow beats orange, blue beats yellow, and green beats blue.\nL15: \nL16: It therefore seems as if the green die has the highest chance of winning and the orange die has the lowest. But now for the surprise: if you roll the green die with the orange die, then two thirds of the time it is the orange die that will win!\nL17: These are known as non-transitive dice. Transitive means that if A beats B and B beats C then A beats C. We see that these dice do not have this property. One way to visualise this is to arrange the dice in a circle, such that each die beats the previous one.\nL18: \nL19: This is rather like the game of “rock, paper, scissors” in which scissors beats paper, and rock beats scissors, but paper beats rock.\nL20: \nL21: A simple game\nL22: You can use this non-transitive property to play a game with a friend. Invite them to examine the dice and then to select any one of them. Without telling them the secret, you now select the next die in the sequence, and then you make, say, 9 rolls against your friend, and keep note of how many times each of you rolls the higher number. Over a sequence of 9 rolls it is very likely that you will roll a higher number more often than your friend.\nL23: \nL24: How the dice work\nL25: cite2†Image: dice_diagram-rgb Look first at the orange die, and notice that it has four copies of the number 2 and two copies of the number 6. Two-thirds of the time, when we roll the orange die it will give a 2, and one third of the time it will give a 6. Therefore, if we roll the orange die against the yellow die (which always gives a 3), the yellow die will, on average, win two-thirds of the time, and will lose one-third of the time.\nL26: You can repeat this game several times, each time allowing your friend to choose their die first.\nL27: Now look at the blue die, and notice that it has four copies of the number 4, and two copies of the number 0. When we roll it against the yellow die, it will therefore give a 4 two thirds of the time, in which case it wins, and a 0 one-third of the time, in which case it loses.\nL28: Now suppose we roll the green die against the blue die. The green die has three copies of the number 1 and three copies of the number 5. To work out the probability that the green die will win we first note that there is a probability of 1/2 that the green die will give a 5, in which case it is certain to win against the blue die. Likewise, there is a probability of 1/2 that the green die will give a 1, in which case there is a probability of 1/3 that it will win.\nL29: The overall probability that the green die will win is then given by multiplying the probabilities:\nL30: cite3†Image L31: \nL32: Finally, consider the probability of the orange die winning against the green die. There is a probability of 1/3 that the orange die will produce a 6, in which case it is certain that the orange die will win. There is similarly a probability of 2/3 that the orange die will produce a 2 in which case there is a 1/2 chance that the orange die will win. The overall probability of the orange die winning is again obtained by multiplying the probabilities:\nL33: \nL34: cite4†Image L35: \nL36: Further information\nL37: Warren Buffett once challenged Bill Gates to a game of dice. “Buffett suggested that each of them choose one of the dice, then discard the other two. They would bet on who would roll the higher number most often. Buffett offered to let Gates pick his die first. This suggestion instantly aroused Gates’ curiosity. He asked to examine the dice, after which he demanded that Buffett choose first.” Buffett was using a set of non-transitive dice!\nL38: \nL39: Opens in a new tab\nL40: \nL41: Follow us:\nL42:   * cite5†Follow on X†x.com L43:   * cite6†Like on Facebook†www.facebook.com L44:   * cite7†Follow on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L45:   * cite8†Subscribe on Youtube†www.youtube.com L46:   * cite9†Follow on Instagram†www.instagram.com L47:   * cite10†Subscribe to our RSS feed L48: \nL49: Share this page:\nL50: \nL51:   * cite11†Share on X†x.com L52:   * cite12†Share on Facebook†www.facebook.com L53:   * cite13†Share on LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L54:   * cite14†Share on Reddit†www.reddit.com L55: cite15†Surface Laptop Ultra cite16†Surface Pro cite17†Surface Laptop cite18†Surface RTX Spark Dev Box cite19†Explore Copilot cite20†Explore Microsoft products cite21†Windows 11 apps cite22†Account profile†account.microsoft.com cite23†Download Center cite24†Microsoft Store support†go.microsoft.com cite25†Returns cite26†Order tracking cite27†Certified Refurbished cite28†Why buy from Microsoft Store cite29†Flexible Payments cite30†Microsoft in education cite31†Devices for education cite32†Microsoft Teams for Education cite33†Microsoft 365 Education cite34†How to buy for your school cite35†Educator training and development†education.microsoft.com cite36†Deals for students and parents cite37†AI for education L56: cite38†Microsoft AI cite39†Microsoft Security cite40†Dynamics 365 cite41†Microsoft 365 cite42†Microsoft Power Platform cite43†Microsoft Teams cite44†Microsoft Copilot cite45†Small Business cite46†Azure†azure.microsoft.com cite47†Microsoft Developer†developer.microsoft.com cite48†Microsoft Learn†learn.microsoft.com cite49†Support for AI marketplace apps cite50†Microsoft Tech Community†techcommunity.microsoft.com cite51†Microsoft Marketplace†marketplace.microsoft.com cite52†Software companies cite53†Visual Studio†visualstudio.microsoft.com cite54†Careers†careers.microsoft.com cite55†About Microsoft cite56†Company news†news.microsoft.com cite57†Privacy at Microsoft cite58†Investors cite59†Diversity and inclusion cite60†Accessibility cite61†Sustainability L57: cite62†Your Privacy Choices†aka.ms cite62†Your Privacy Choices†aka.ms L58: \nL59: cite63†Consumer Health Privacy†go.microsoft.com cite64†Sitemap cite65†Contact Microsoft†support.microsoft.com cite66†Privacy†go.microsoft.com cite67†Manage cookies cite68†Terms of use†go.microsoft.com cite69†Trademarks†go.microsoft.com cite70†Safety & eco†go.microsoft.com cite71†Recycling cite72†About our ads†choice.microsoft.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChecking your browser - reCAPTCHA (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4751343/)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4751343/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\nL0: Checking your browser before accessing pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ...\nL1: \nL2: Click cite0†here if you are not automatically redirected after 5 seconds.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.cs.tufts.edu/comp/250P/classpages/birthday.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.cs.tufts.edu/comp/250P/classpages/birthday.html: (400) Timeout fetching\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n24.42 -- Cone rolls uphill (https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~lecturedemonstrations/Composer/Pages/24.42.html)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~lecturedemonstrations/Composer/Pages/24.42.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 20\nL0: cite0†Image: Cone uphill, cylinder down L1: \nL2: cite1†A video of this demonstration is available at this link.†youtu.be L3: \nL4: On this V-shaped ramp, the cylinder rolls downhill, to the left, but the double cone rolls uphill, to the right.\nL5: > The center of mass of an object is the weighted mean displacement of all of its mass points from some reference point, usually the origin of the coordinate system in which the object sits. This is the sum of the products of all the mass points with their distances from the origin, divided by the total mass of the object (r_{cm} = Σm_{i}r_{i}/M).\nL6: It is usually most convenient to set the center of mass of the object at the origin, but whatever our reference point, for both the cylinder and the double cone in this demonstration, the center of mass lies on the central axis, equidistant from both ends.\nL7: >\nL8: > If the object is relatively small, and close to the earth, then gravity acts uniformly on the object. That is, if the object is of uniform density, gravity exerts the same downward force on all of its mass points, and the sum of all of these forces is equal to a single downward force, F = Mg, acting at the center of mass of the object. This point is thus also called the center of gravity.\nL9: >\nL10: > When an object is situated so that, when gravity acts on it, by moving in a particular direction it can lower its center of mass and thus lower its gravitational potential energy, then the object moves in that direction. A familiar example is a ball rolling down a hill.\nL11: >\nL12: > The V-shaped ramp is made so that its vertex lies lower than its wide end, so that the downhill direction is from the wide end to the vertex. As noted above, for both the cylinder and the double cone, the center of mass lies on the central axis, equidistant from both ends. If you place the cylinder at the wide end of the ramp, it rolls down the ramp, as we should expect.\nL13: Since the cylinder has the same radius over its entire length, as it rolls on the ramp, its center of mass stays the same level above the ramp. Thus, as it rolls down the ramp from the high end to the low end, it lowers its center of mass, and thus also its gravitational potential energy.\nL14: >\nL15: > If, however, you place the double cone at the wide end of the ramp, it merely sits there. At this end of the ramp, the double cone rests on the rails of the ramp near its ends, where its radius is smallest. If you roll the double cone down the ramp towards its vertex, as the rails get closer together, they support the double cone closer and closer to its center, where its radius is largest, so that the radius of the cone where the rails support it increases as it rolls down the ramp.\nL16: The cone angle and the angle between the rails are such that the rate of this increase in radius exceeds the slope of the ramp. Rolling the double cone in this direction thus raises its center of mass and its gravitational potential energy. This means that if you place the double cone at the low end of the ramp, where it rests on the rails near its center, its center of mass and its gravitational potential energy are higher than they are when the cone is at the high end of the ramp.\nL17: The double cone thus rolls along the ramp in the uphill direction, because as it does so, the height of its center of mass decreases, and so does its gravitational potential energy.\nL18: >\nL19: >\n"
